WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.440
It was three a.m and I was still in the
office.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.230
Deliberately working on my perfect
PowerPoint slides.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.920
I was still in the office because my team
and

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.310
I had this big presentation to an
important client the next day.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.290
And we want to get it just perfect.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:33.220
However, our presentation was just in six
hours and with every hour that

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:39.000
passed I actually wondered if what I was
still doing was beneficial or,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.730
quite frankly detrimental, to our ability
to deliver to the client the next day.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:45.620
And don't get me wrong like, I'm a type A.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.140
I was gonna get this presentation perfect.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.310
But I also understood that spending an
extra hour in the office that night

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:54.560
meant an, less, one hour's sleep less.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:01.187
And as I was battling through this idea of
trade offs,

00:01:01.187 --> 00:01:07.200
I realized I had been in that situation on
numerous occasions before,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.420
but that wasn't in the office, it was on
the tennis court.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:17.060
Before I came to the GSB I worked as a
management consultant.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.430
But prior to that, I was a tennis player.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:24.090
And while sports and business may seem
worlds apart for

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:28.230
you, there are surprisingly many parallels
and

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.800
today I would like to explore three of
them with you.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:35.180
The three that represent the most
important lessons,

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.340
I have gotten out of sports.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.430
So the first lesson is about preparation.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:47.900
And for that, let's all imagine we are
world class tennis players.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.360
Everyone.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.470
So, you are playing at Wimbledon semi
finals, your serve has been like

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:59.170
completely off this whole game, but with
this last point you go up to the line,

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:03.070
you serve, and it's an ace, and you won.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.980
You just won semi-finalist of Wimbledon.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:10.225
You'll go off to the net, you thank your
opponent, you do the press conference.

00:02:10.225 --> 00:02:12.025
It's always the same questions, the same
answers.

00:02:12.025 --> 00:02:13.475
So you're gonna see something along the
lines,

00:02:13.475 --> 00:02:16.925
it was a very tough match, my opponent
played great.

00:02:16.925 --> 00:02:18.540
I'm very happy to be in the finals.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:24.115
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, but now what?

00:02:24.115 --> 00:02:28.345
You have 24 hours until probably the
biggest day of your life,

00:02:28.345 --> 00:02:30.565
finals of the world's oldest tournament.

00:02:30.565 --> 00:02:31.105
What do you do?

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:36.930
Well then we tell you even if that serve
felt a little bit off today,

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.210
you're not going to practice.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:40.770
Your body is already exhausted.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.110
I'm about to fall off.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:47.000
So what you need to do is actually get
your body and your mind ready for

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.600
another five hour battle, by jogging,
stretching, Ice bath, massage.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:58.120
You're likely to go over your strategy
with your coach, maybe visualize,

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.110
if that's something, if that's what works
for you, but that's it.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.210
Now let's think about it in terms of
business.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.830
We also have those big days in our lives,
don't we?

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:16.620
A board presentation, a client meeting,
final round interview, and yes, I get it.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.690
We're not athletes, but fundamentally,
we're all human beings,

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:26.340
and it's very interesting to me that we
tend to think that the more we work,

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.529
the more energy we have, the less we
sleep, the fresher our brain is.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:32.380
Right?

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.290
I'll tell you on that day or actually
rather say night at three a.m

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:42.470
in the morning, I intuitively knew that
what I was doing was wrong.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.648
That staying out that one night was really
not going to move the needle.

00:03:46.648 --> 00:03:52.500
But this temptation to have this excuse to
be able to say,

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:56.805
we've worked as hard as we could because
we worked until the very last minute,

00:03:56.805 --> 00:03:58.850
aas hard to resist.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:03.260
But the truth is whether it's sports or
business,

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.410
one cannot deliver truly great performance
by cramming on a final night.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:13.420
Because the preparation for a big day
starts long before.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.560
So next time, you have this big day coming
up,

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:23.260
prepare as much as you can until the day
before and

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:27.010
give yourself some rest, get a good
night's sleep, and don't get me wrong.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:31.020
This is not about work life balance, this
is not about being soft on yourself,

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:35.760
it's about being strategic and maximizing
your chances of performing on the big day.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:43.220
Okay, so story number two is about
responding to things we don't control.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:48.080
So let me give you a quick story from my
college career.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.380
It was my sophomore year, the first game
of the season.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.110
So everyone is in the stands and you can
feel that excitement.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.540
And as I'm hitting the ball, I feel this
shooting pain in my wrist.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.160
No treatment helps over next few days and

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.800
the next thing I know, I'm in cast and out
for half of the season.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:15.270
And this, just doesn't feel fair.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:16.060
It doesn't feel right.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.980
At that time in my life, tennis wasn't
everything, but it almost was.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:29.330
I remember I drove back and, from the
hospital, my coach sat me down and

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:35.280
he said Claude's, there are things you
don't control, such as your injury.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:38.940
But it is up to you to respond to that
situation.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:43.030
The team will need you at the end of the
season.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:43.970
But you need to be ready.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:49.780
Now, being ready meant I needed to do
conditioning and running everyday.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:53.021
There's one thing I hated about tennis,
that's it.

00:05:53.021 --> 00:05:54.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:58.780
&gt;&gt; So, not only I, did I need to something
I hated the most,

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:00.730
I actually had no guarantees it was gonna
help.

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:04.030
Because quite frankly, we didn't know what
was gonna happen down the line,

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:05.260
if I was gonna be able to play.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:11.420
So I'll tell you it felt tempting to
forfeit.

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:13.260
To give up the season due to injury.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:15.960
But it didn't.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.030
And so I started running, biking,

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:21.460
day in, day out, they soon removed my cast
and I got back on the court.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:26.390
I lost my first match after coming back.

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:27.650
But at the end of the season,

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:31.950
when it mattered, I helped the team win
the conference tournament and

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:37.140
I clinched our win to qualify to Sweet 16,
equivalent of players for Super Bowl.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:40.070
Looking back, those were,

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:44.850
that was the best season we've had as a
team in my four years at William and Mary.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.180
And more important than the results were
the lessons,

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:50.540
intangible lessons, they learn about team
dynamics.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.470
Because not being able to play gave me
insights into team dynamics

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:56.280
I wouldn't otherwise be able to observe.

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:59.080
Let me tell you a secret,

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:02.845
varsity tennis team really not that
different from McKinsey project team.

00:07:02.845 --> 00:07:03.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:09.030
&gt;&gt; So, athletes don't control injuries.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:12.420
But, we as business professionals also
don't control everything.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:18.770
That project you didn't really want to be
staffed on, guess what, you're doing it.

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:22.760
You know that client that no one wants to
work with?

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:25.150
You're assigned to him.

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:28.890
Or that job promotion that you really
wanted, but you didn't get it.

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:35.100
And at those times, it may feel tempting
to forfeit, to give up.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:38.720
But it is up to you to make most of that
situation.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:45.160
So when you face that situation, I'd love
for you to remember words of John Wooden,

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:48.440
probably the most famous coach in the
history of college sports who said,

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:56.040
when things go bad for reasons we don't
control, it is very tempting to first

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:00.810
blame and then embrace fate as a reason
for our failure.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:03.130
But this is not what effective leaders do.

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:10.320
Okay so my last story is about getting a
support team.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:13.920
So let's now all go back to Wimbledon.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:14.820
You are playing that final.

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:18.300
So you are already like on the central
court and you are warming up and

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:24.840
as you are warming up, you glance over to
your box, and you see your coach there,

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:28.300
your trainer, your hitting partner, along
with your friends and family.

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:31.580
And you realize how lucky you are.

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:36.020
You are here because you've worked really
hard.

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:39.540
But you're also here because of their hard
work and their support.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.570
Now, is this really that much different
from business?

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:49.120
I'll argue no, our ability to succeed in a
business place will depend on

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:52.860
our hard work but also on the strength of
our support team.

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:58.200
Yet for some reason, when we talk about
coaching in athletics,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.020
it's normally obvious, but

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:03.030
it gets more uncomfortable when we talk
about it in the world of business.

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:10.600
I remember a few months ago, the CEO of a
Fortune 500 company came to our class and

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:13.200
he talked about working with coaches and
counselors.

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:18.170
When we reflected back on his presentation
you know,

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:21.330
some of us expressed admiration for him
sharing the story.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:24.510
But some people were outwardly uneasy.

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:29.660
I remember one person saying you know,

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:36.440
if I knew that my coach was seeing or my
boss was seeing a coach or a counselor,

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:40.030
I'm not sure if I wanted to work for him
if I would have faith for him.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:44.010
And that was so interesting to me.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:46.600
Because in sports, exactly opposite.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:49.110
It's not only normal, it is actually
crucial to your success.

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:56.980
The CEO ended his talk by saying, if you
could get help, why wouldn't you?

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:01.060
If you could become a better leader.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:01.690
Why wouldn't you?

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:04.310
Why wouldn't you?

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:09.970
Today I've talked to you about parallels
between worlds of sports and

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:10.790
worlds of business.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:16.430
I've told you how tennis players go about
winning their game.

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:19.380
And if I were to summarize my whole talk
in just one word.

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:21.940
It would be ownership.

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:27.875
Own your preparation, own your response,
and own your support system.

00:10:27.875 --> 00:10:31.428
I hope some of these lessons resonated
with you, and

00:10:31.428 --> 00:10:33.625
will help you win your own game.

00:10:33.625 --> 00:10:34.334
Thank you.

00:10:34.334 --> 00:10:35.042
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:35.042 --> 00:10:39.389
[MUSIC]

